00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.130
So let's talk a little bit
about preventing OS tampering.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:54.530
And this is another
very unique thing

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:57.200
that Google's able to
do with Chrome OS that

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.230
enhances its security
beyond traditional OSes.

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:03.440
Verified boot is a manner in
which the Google hardware,

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.180
along with the
firmware and the OS,

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.030
is able to check against
the known image that

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:09.890
is made and provided
by Google that

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:14.850
is in that known stable state
as it was originally deployed.

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:19.290
So in this example here, if
the OS was to be tampered with,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.240
it would not pass verified boot.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.830
When it doesn't
pass verified boot,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:24.990
the device is not
booting, and the data's

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:28.150
not available to users.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.970
And at that point, the device
either needs to be reimaged,

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:34.225
or wiped so it could be
restored to factory defaults.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:38.750
So let's also talk
about Chrome OS

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:41.840
and how it actually reduces
your data footprint.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.720
Chrome OS devices are
unique in which data is not

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.735
traditionally stored on an OS.

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:48.110
