00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:25.280
and you can deploy Android
applications to your Chrome OS

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.490
device.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:29.570
You can also access the Chrome
browser, which is a popular way

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.680
to access web applications.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.170
Finally, the Chrome Web Store--

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:34.640
these are extensions
that you can

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:39.060
install onto your Chrome browser
and your Chrome OS device.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:42.200
And most recently is running
native Linux applications.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.870
Linux can run within a container
on your Chrome OS device,

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:46.910
and you can deploy Linux
applications to them.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.770
So let's talk about sandboxing.

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:55.190
Sandboxing is something that
is very unique to Google Chrome

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:59.900
and to Chrome OS that is at the
heart of its security model.

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:04.490
Sandboxing is the concept that
all browser tabs, applications,

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:08.430
and web apps that you
deploy to your devices

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:10.430
are contained within
themselves, and they do not

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:13.590
have access to the OS and
to other applications.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.880
This is a model that makes sure
that your applications cannot

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:18.800
access your OS, cannot
access your data,

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:21.950
and applications cannot
cross-talk to one another.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:23.750
And site isolation,
on top of this,

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:27.320
keeps all the processes within
each browser tab separate.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:30.680
Part of this methodology is what
keeps your Chrome OS device so

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.920
secure-- that even if you do
hit a malicious site, that it

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.080
only stays within
that one sandbox,

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.700
and is not able to affect other
areas of your Chrome device.

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:46.710
The Chrome Web Store is a very
popular Chrome marketplace

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:49.320
where you can download
extensions and get

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.860
various applications to
deploy to your users.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.330
From an enterprise
management side,

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:56.910
you can push those
applications out to your users,

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:01.080
and users also have the ability
to manually install them.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:04.530
Chrome Web Store applications
also have permissions that they

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:06.420
will effect on
your Chrome device,

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.970
and as an administrator, you
have the ability to block

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.640
permissions so certain
applications cannot access

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.470
certain areas of
the Chrome device,

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:16.280
such as maybe
blocking a USB port.

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:18.830
And finally, other
Chrome security measures

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:23.120
have been in place to disable
the install of extensions.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.860
So extensions can only be
installed from Google's Chrome

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.970
Web Store, where
machine learning

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:31.070
and artificial
intelligence is used

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.110
to ensure those
applications deployed do not

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:34.070
contain any malware.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.120
So management of Chrome devices
is a very popular method

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.350
to managing the various
policies on your device.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:47.650
There's over 200 policies
available in the console.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:51.700
And at this time, some of the
blacklisting and whitelisting

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.790
of extensions and applications
are widely used by enterprises

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:56.885
to pick and choose
applications that they

00:14:56.885 --> 00:14:58.510
deploy in their
environment, whether it

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:00.640
be to users or to devices.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.360
You also have the ability to
blacklist URLs and prevent

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:07.090
users from accessing
sites that you have

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:09.770
forbidden as an organization.

00:15:09.770 --> 00:15:11.800
And finally, the
Password Alert Policy

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.610
is something that you can employ
to ensure that your users don't

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:19.570
use their corporate password
in noncorporate authorized

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:20.500
websites.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:25.000
So this can ensure that their
corporate-owned passwords

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.510
do not become phished or
utilized in other areas outside

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:32.320
of the corporate environment.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:37.120
So Android apps on Chromebooks
is an ever-growing environment

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:40.300
to deploy applications
on your Chrome devices.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:47.020
Play Protect is
a proprietary way

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:50.800
that Google inspects the
applications within the Android

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:51.430
market.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:53.920
And it scans and
removes applications

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:56.500
every single day
from the marketplace,

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:59.890
ensuring that you're
only deploying known,

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:02.030
good applications
to your environment.

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:03.520
And additionally,
as an Enterprise

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.020
feature as an
administrator, you can

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:07.420
build a curated
list of applications

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:09.910
that your users can
then choose to install.

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.980
And as an administrator,
you can also

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.230
choose to deny
applications, as well,

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.150
that shouldn't be installed.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:18.850
And finally, each
individual application

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.640
may also have individual,
specific app policies

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:23.230
that you can apply
to that application

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:25.315
as well, whether it be
configuration or security

00:16:25.315 --> 00:16:26.440
settings of the app itself.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:32.440
Now, let's go over
some protection

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:35.830
against current threats and
how we can keep our users

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.510
and our data safe and secure.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:44.030
So phishing is a very common
attack vector currently.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.070
And phishing is the
concept that an email

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:49.730
is sent to a user that looks
legitimate, and is ultimately

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:54.050
getting them to click to
proceed to another website

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:57.050
or application or to
download software,

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.290
but it is not what the
user is actually expecting.

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:03.080
It is malicious software,
and it is ultimately

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.359
trying to either
exfiltrate their data,

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:08.150
or gain access to their systems.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:09.950
So Google Safe
Browsing is one method

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:13.010
you can use to protect
yourself against these threats.

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:15.530
And at Google, we use what's
called a security keys,

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:17.960
but essentially, a second
form factor for your identity

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.030
can be another popular
method to protect

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:21.410
your users from phishing.

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:24.680
So even if they do willingly
give out their password,

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:28.690
the attacker won't have the
second form factor available.

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:30.690
And finally, if the
attack prevails,

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:32.710
implementing the
Password Alert Policy

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.470
so both users and
administrators can be aware

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.090
that a corporate
password has been

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:40.370
used on a site that isn't
owned or corporately managed.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.730
So ransomware is a
little bit different.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:49.750
It's designed to block
access to a computer system

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:54.310
until a sum of money is paid or
negotiations have been required

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:58.520
to gain access back to your
system to recover your data.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:02.490
The Chromebook data model
is completely different,

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:06.210
and is really low on
device data footprint,

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.970
meaning there's no data
on a Chromebook, really,

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:12.330
to hold ransom, because the
data isn't on the device.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:15.310
The Chrome OS is a
read-only operating system,

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:18.860
meaning that you can't install
software to affect the OS--

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:22.710
to infect it or install
software that may do this.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.580
And finally, if ransomware
did exist for Chromebooks,

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.370
the verified boot
method would ensure

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:31.130
that it was not
able to be infected

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:35.690
and would not pass
verified boot methods.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:39.250
And finally is Chromebooks
don't run traditional Windows

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:42.130
operating systems and
executables, making them

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:45.460
therefore a inhospitable
place to operate

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:49.940
within the ransomware
environment.

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:53.760
So other common attack vectors
are malicious applications.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.020
And these could be
applications that ultimately

00:18:57.020 --> 00:19:00.080
look to exfiltrate data
from your environment

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.200
or from your users.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:05.440
And permission-based
blocking is a popular method

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:08.290
to ensure that these
applications don't have access

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:09.380
to your data.

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:11.740
So whether it's
blocking access to URLs

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:16.030
or blocking access to
portions of USB ports

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:17.650
and things of that
nature, you're

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:21.040
able to blacklist
or block permissions

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.590
to applications as well.

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:25.840
And Managed Google
Play also then lets

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:29.000
you just curate that list of
applications for your users

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:31.620
so your users aren't using
applications that haven't

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:35.450
been approved for by IT.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:38.560
And finally, if
attacks do happen,

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:40.930
remember, we have the
sandboxing environment,

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:44.190
so any attack only happens
within that contained sandbox

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.080
or application
environment, and doesn't

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.645
affect the OS and
other applications

00:19:47.645 --> 00:19:49.020
running on your
Chrome OS device.

00:19:51.775 --> 00:19:53.150
So this is a very
common question

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:56.460
we get from enterprises as
they're new into Chrome OS--

00:19:56.460 --> 00:20:00.560
is why Chromebooks don't require
antivirus or malware software.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:02.840
So one is really,
again, is Chrome OS

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:06.200
was designed from the ground-up
as a secure operating system.

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:07.850
It is a read-only
operating system,

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:09.860
and applications
cannot be installed,

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:13.910
and they cannot modify the OS.,
therefore limiting the ability

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:16.850
of what antivirus software
would be able to check for.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:20.360
Sandboxing isolates
applications so they can only

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:23.780
attack within their own
controlled environment.

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:26.060
It goes a little bit further
that any virus software

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:28.460
typically wants to be able
to scan entire systems,

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:30.590
and Chrome OS is not
a scannable system,

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:33.590
nor would have access to
scanners of other applications.

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:36.920
Verified boot is
typically another method

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.130
to prevent tampering of devices,
and if a device is tampered

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:43.790
with, it won't be
bootable, and the user

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:47.570
would have to wipe the device
in order to regain access to it,

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:50.010
and wiping any
data along with it.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:53.150
And finally is there
is the ability,

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:56.550
as an administrator,
to curate and manage

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.560
your control list of
extensions and apps

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:02.190
to your Chrome devices, further
limiting the attack vector

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.625
and the applications that
will run in your environment.

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:12.070
So why are Chromebook
updates so effective?

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:15.390
I'd like to say it's because
one update does so many things.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.940
The Chrome update is very
unique in the ability

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.890
that it updates your
firmware, your operating

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:22.650
system, and your
application with one update.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:25.920
There's not separate updates
for patching applications,

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:28.850
or patching OS and
patching firmware.

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:30.950
The update happens
in the background,

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:34.172
it is seamless to the user,
and it is a 6-second reboot.

00:21:34.172 --> 00:21:35.630
In fact, in many
cases, users don't

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.130
realize they've been updated.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:41.400
And finally, there are ways
of managing the updates

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.800
through the
Administrative Console

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:46.500
so you can selectively
choose when updates

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.080
are delivered to
your users, and what

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:50.550
devices receive the updates.

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:59.720
So Chromebooks are really
a trusted business laptop

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:04.040
in environments to keeping
enterprises' data secure

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:06.010
as they move to the cloud.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:08.990
And I'm going to be going
over a couple of customers

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:12.900
that are using Chrome in
their environment today.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:15.170
So let's talk
about Duo Security.

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:19.310
Duo Security is
a digital company

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:22.880
focused on digital security
and trust compliance,

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:24.650
and they were looking
for a solution

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:30.380
for managing their data and
ensuring that it's secure

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:32.750
no matter where the
device is being used

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:35.220
or where the device is deployed.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:38.150
So the real solution
that they were after

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:40.220
is that it's secure by
design, that they did not

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.740
have to layer on
third-party software

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.630
in order to manage
their devices.

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:47.210
And they also were able to get
a premium device experience

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:50.250
using Google Pixel hardware.

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:51.620
So it offers them--

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:54.650
in their words, it offered a
more effective endpoint control

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:56.030
and security.

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:59.360
The extensions and the Web
Store offered a wide array

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:01.130
of applications and
services that they

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:03.350
could deploy and manage.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:06.080
And they also mentioned
that their premium devices,

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:08.990
such as a Google
Pixel, rival over

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.620
other competitors' devices.

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:14.470
And again, suitable for both
public-facing and internal use

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:14.970
cases.

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:20.260
So Charles Schwab is
another enterprise

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:22.260
that uses Chromebooks.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:24.010
In addition to
Chrome OS customer,

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:26.170
they are a highly-regulated
environment

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:29.650
and a very
security-minded industry.

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:32.230
And one of the reasons that
they've chose the platform

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:35.530
is the settings that it
afforded to their administrators

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:37.420
in order to manage
their devices.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:39.490
Chrome OS devices
and Chrome management

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:42.220
is a much more simplistic
way of managing endpoint

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:44.830
devices than traditional OSes.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:46.990
It gives your administrators
more time and freedom

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:50.314
to do work that has higher
value than managing endpoints.

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:56.580
And of course, you can
always learn more by going

00:23:56.580 --> 00:24:01.710
to cloud.google.com
/chromeenterprisesecurity

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:05.180
to learn more about
Chrome Enterprise.

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:07.490
And I want to thank
everyone for their time.

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:09.720
And if you'll just
please stay tuned,

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:11.630
we're going to be
going over the live Q&amp;A

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:12.920
questions in just a moment.

