of Chrome devices?

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:41.010
Chromebooks can be managed
through the Chrome Enterprise

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:43.800
Administrative Console,
and Google updates

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:45.420
can be managed
through that console.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:48.660
You're able to organize your
devices into a series of OUs

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:51.540
where, from there, you're able
to set policies that control

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:55.540
the updates, including
one, if you want,

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:57.360
choose to have
auto-updates, or choose

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:58.710
to not have auto-updates.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.193
At Google, we call
that [? pinning. ?]

00:27:01.193 --> 00:27:03.360
It is best practice to
remain [? on ?] auto-updates,

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:05.700
obviously, for best security
reasons and application

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:06.370
performance.

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:08.370
But as an administrator,
you do have the ability

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:10.165
to pause those updates as well.

00:27:10.165 --> 00:27:11.790
There's also a number
of other settings

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.890
that allow you to
stagger the updates,

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:15.570
and also control,
as we mentioned,

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:17.550
the Dev, Beta, and
Stable channel,

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:18.870
as well, out to your fleet.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.420
So there's lots of options to
controlling those updates out

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:24.320
to your environment.

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:27.310
Let me go to question number 2.

00:27:27.310 --> 00:27:29.320
Are the security
benefits mentioned

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.200
in this deck available
on all Chrome devices

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:33.770
with all manufacturers?

00:27:33.770 --> 00:27:35.830
It's a great question.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:39.160
So Chrome OS is an
operating system

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:42.580
made by Google that is placed
on manufacturer hardware.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:46.210
The manufacturer hardware
is negotiated with Google,

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:49.520
and contains all the proprietary
components like we mentioned--

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.790
the TPM module and that
tamper-proof-resistant

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:52.660
firmware--

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:54.340
inside of every
single Chrome device.

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:57.130
So whether you pick a
Chromebook or a Chromebox

00:27:57.130 --> 00:28:01.290
or other Chrome form factors,
and hardware-independent.

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:04.980
Whether it is Google-made
hardware by another major OEM,

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:09.030
they all contain the same
underlying security benefits

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:11.250
that you receive, and
the operating system

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:16.090
is authored by Google
for all those devices.

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:18.670
So we have another
question here.

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.430
This question's
asking about G Suite.

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:23.050
So G Suite is a popular
series of applications,

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:27.150
including Email, Docs,
Sheets, and Calendar.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.610
So it says, do I need
to be a G Suite customer

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.490
to get all the benefits
of Chrome Enterprise?

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:35.070
I would-- no, you do not
need to be a G Suite customer

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.090
to get the benefits
of Chrome enterprises.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:41.280
We have lots of enterprises that
are on traditional productivity

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.620
applications, and are
not G Suite customers,

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:45.750
and are very successful
using Chrome OS.

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.780
They're able to deploy their
applications through the Cloud

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:52.940
Console and manage them
in a similar manner,

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:55.620
but simpler than their
traditional Windows devices.

00:28:55.620 --> 00:29:00.720
And their applications can
also be deployed virtually

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:04.740
if there are dependencies
on Windows applications.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:06.360
And that goes into
our next question,

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:10.110
asking, how will we run PC
applications on a Chromebook?

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:12.465
So PC applications, or
executable applications,

00:29:12.465 --> 00:29:16.350
[? EACs, ?] do not run
natively on a Chromebook.

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:20.370
What organizations typically
do is virtualize applications

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.200
and deliver them to
their Chromebook.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:25.590
So there's many popular
virtualization platforms.

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:28.620
Google has partnerships
with Citrix and VMware

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:30.900
to deliver those
virtual applications

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:33.030
onto your Chromebook,
but we work

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:37.077
with many other virtualization
platforms that are deployed

00:29:37.077 --> 00:29:38.160
on the Chromebook as well.

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:42.890
So let me go on to
our next question.

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:45.040
So what are common
applications customers

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:47.930
deploy to their end users?

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:50.230
This is a very good
question, also very broad.

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:52.150
But there's a series
of applications

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:55.532
that I'd say are very common
to deploy on a Chromebook.

00:29:55.532 --> 00:29:57.490
Some of the most popular
applications, I think,

00:29:57.490 --> 00:29:59.590
are ones that
organizations use daily,

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:02.350
such as video conferencing
and teleconferencing

00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:06.430
systems, such as WebEx and
Zoom and Google Hangouts.

00:30:06.430 --> 00:30:09.550
But other applications, such
as Android applications,

00:30:09.550 --> 00:30:12.430
are also very popular
to deploy, including

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:15.010
products by Cisco and Palo
Alto and other networking

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:17.650
applications that they may
use, and other productivity

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:21.140
software such as Microsoft
Office, as well as Google G

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:21.640
Suite.

00:30:24.435 --> 00:30:25.810
And again, all
those applications

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:27.970
can be deployed through
the Management Console,

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:29.770
and are deployed through
the Cloud Console,

00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:31.062
and then pushed to the devices.

00:30:33.790 --> 00:30:35.750
So we have another
question here--

00:30:35.750 --> 00:30:37.967
this is a little more
security-oriented--

00:30:37.967 --> 00:30:39.550
about can we explain
a little bit more

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:41.270
about what ephemeral mode is?

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:43.810
So we mentioned, in the deck,
about a unique feature--

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:45.820
security feature--
called Ephemeral Mode.

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:48.010
Ephemeral Mode is
unique to Chrome OS,

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:50.260
and it gives it the ability
that every time a user

00:30:50.260 --> 00:30:53.590
enters and exits their session,
that all the local data

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:55.353
within their profile is white.

00:30:55.353 --> 00:30:56.770
So whether it's
downloaded content

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:59.810
that they put in their
Downloads folder,

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:02.120
cached data,
passwords-- anything

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.730
that may have been saved
during their user session

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:07.670
is wiped on the exit
of their session,

00:31:07.670 --> 00:31:11.240
and every new session
created is a blank profile.

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:13.580
And no data is
stored or captured,

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:15.590
and is wiped clean every time.

00:31:15.590 --> 00:31:17.870
This is, as we referred
to back in the deck,

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:19.280
keeping a low data footprint.

00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:21.500
So it's a very popular
method for organizations

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:23.390
to employ Ephemeral
Mode, because they

00:31:23.390 --> 00:31:28.790
can be ensured that there is no
data left behind on the device.

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:31.220
And our last question here
is a little bit on networking

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:32.690
and security tools.

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:34.310
What networking
and security tools

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:37.280
are available for Chromebooks?

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:40.580
Most of the major manufacturers
of networking applications

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:44.410
provide applications both in
the Web Store-- the Chrome Web

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:46.160
Store-- and on
the Android market

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:47.390
for networking applications.

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:51.870
So whether it's VPN applications
or proxy applications,

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:53.660
most of the major
manufacturers are

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:56.060
available within the
Google Web Store,

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:57.640
and on the Android Play Store.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:31:59.620
I'd say the more popular
ones are obviously

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:02.380
Cisco and Palo Alto,
but many others

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:06.820
exist from the other
network providers as well.

00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:09.330
So I think that wraps up
our questions that we've

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:11.070
gotten from our audience.

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:13.280
I just want to thank
everyone for tuning in,

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:16.230
and to please visit Cloud
OnAir to discover more content

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:17.767
from Google Cloud experts.

00:32:17.767 --> 00:32:18.600
Thank you very much.

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:21.950
